<DocsLayout title="Built-in functions" description="Reference for thinDB scalar and aggregate SQL functions." headings={headings}>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="SQL reference" title="Built-in functions" description="The shipped registry covers strings, regular expressions, URLs, math, dates, JSON, network addresses, arrays, maps, lambdas, bitmaps, conversion, conditional logic, and exact and statistical aggregates." />
  <h2 id="scalar-functions">Scalar functions</h2>
  <p>Function names are case-insensitive in SQL. The tables below list the public names present in the scalar registry; aliases are shown separately because they may be useful when porting MySQL or PostgreSQL queries.</p>

  <h3 id="string">String and binary</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Functions</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Case and trim</td><td><code>upper</code>, <code>ucase</code>, <code>lower</code>, <code>lcase</code>, <code>initcap</code>, <code>trim</code>, <code>ltrim</code>, <code>rtrim</code>, and <code>TRIM([LEADING | TRAILING | BOTH] chars FROM s)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Length and bytes</td><td><code>length</code>, <code>char_length</code>, <code>octet_length</code>, <code>bit_length</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Compose</td><td><code>concat</code>, <code>concat_ws</code>, <code>repeat</code>, <code>space</code>, <code>lpad</code>, <code>rpad</code>, <code>elt</code>, <code>insert</code>, <code>make_set</code>, <code>quote</code>, <code>append_trailing_char_if_absent</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Slice</td><td><code>substring</code>, <code>left</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>split_part</code>, <code>substring_index</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Search</td><td><code>instr</code>, <code>locate</code>, <code>position</code>, <code>strpos</code>, <code>field</code>, <code>find_in_set</code>, <code>starts_with</code>, <code>ends_with</code>, <code>ngram_search</code>, <code>ngram_search_case_insensitive</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Transform</td><td><code>replace</code>, <code>translate</code>, <code>reverse</code>, <code>strcmp</code>, <code>soundex</code>, <code>null_or_empty</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Regular expressions</td><td><code>regexp_like</code>, <code>regexp_replace</code>, <code>regexp_substr</code>, <code>regexp_extract</code>, <code>regexp_extract_all</code>, <code>regexp_count</code>, <code>regexp_instr</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>URLs</td><td><code>url_encode</code>, <code>url_decode</code>, <code>url_extract_host</code>, <code>url_extract_parameter</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Characters and encodings</td><td><code>ascii</code>, <code>ord</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>chr</code>, <code>hex</code>, <code>unhex</code>, <code>bin</code>, <code>to_base64</code>, <code>from_base64</code>, <code>to_binary</code>, <code>from_binary</code>, <code>conv</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Display</td><td><code>bar</code>, <code>format_bytes</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Hashes</td><td><code>crc32</code>, <code>md5</code>, <code>md5sum</code>, <code>sha1</code>, <code>sha2</code>, <code>sha256</code>, <code>murmur_hash3_32</code>, <code>xx_hash3_64</code>, <code>xx_hash3_128</code>; encryption with <code>aes_encrypt</code> and <code>aes_decrypt</code></td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`SELECT
  upper(trim(name)) AS normalized,
  regexp_replace(path, '/+', '/') AS clean_path,
  substring(description, 1, 80) AS preview,
  sha256(external_id) AS id_hash
FROM imports;`} />

  <h3 id="math">Math and numeric</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Functions</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Basic</td><td><code>abs</code>, <code>sign</code>, <code>ceil</code>, <code>ceiling</code>, <code>floor</code>, <code>round</code>, <code>truncate</code>, <code>mod</code>, <code>pmod</code>, <code>fmod</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Powers and logs</td><td><code>pow</code>, <code>power</code>, <code>square</code>, <code>sqrt</code>, <code>cbrt</code>, <code>exp</code>, <code>ln</code>, <code>log</code>, <code>log10</code>, <code>log2</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Trigonometry</td><td><code>sin</code>, <code>cos</code>, <code>tan</code>, <code>asin</code>, <code>acos</code>, <code>atan</code>, <code>atan2</code>, <code>cot</code>, <code>degrees</code>, <code>radians</code>, <code>pi</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Comparison</td><td><code>greatest</code>, <code>least</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bits</td><td><code>bitand</code>, <code>bitor</code>, <code>bitxor</code>, <code>bitnot</code>, <code>bit_shift_left</code>, <code>bit_shift_right</code>, <code>bit_count</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Buckets and random</td><td><code>equiwidth_bucket</code>, <code>interval(n, n1, n2, ...)</code> (how many of the ascending bounds <code>n</code> reaches), <code>rand</code>, <code>random</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Operator kernels</td><td><code>add</code>, <code>sub</code>, <code>mul</code>, <code>div</code> back SQL arithmetic and are callable by name.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

  <h3 id="date-time">Date and time</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Functions</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Parts</td><td><code>year</code>, <code>quarter</code>, <code>month</code>, <code>monthname</code>, <code>day</code>, <code>dayofmonth</code>, <code>dayofweek</code>, <code>dayofyear</code>, <code>dayname</code>, <code>dayofweek_iso</code>, <code>weekday</code>, <code>week</code>, <code>weekofyear</code>, <code>week_iso</code>, <code>yearweek</code>, <code>hour</code>, <code>minute</code>, <code>second</code>, <code>microsecond</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>time</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Construct and boundaries</td><td><code>makedate</code>, <code>from_days</code>, <code>last_day</code>, <code>next_day</code>, <code>previous_day</code>, <code>date_trunc</code>, and the bucket a time falls in with <code>time_slice</code> and <code>date_slice</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Differences</td><td><code>datediff</code>, <code>date_diff</code>, <code>timestampdiff</code>, <code>timediff</code>, <code>period_diff</code>, <code>to_days</code>, <code>to_seconds</code>, <code>time_to_sec</code>, and <code>a - b</code> in one unit: <code>years_diff</code>, <code>months_diff</code>, <code>weeks_diff</code>, <code>days_diff</code>, <code>hours_diff</code>, <code>minutes_diff</code>, <code>seconds_diff</code>, <code>milliseconds_diff</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Add and subtract</td><td><code>date_add</code>, <code>date_sub</code>, <code>date_add_months</code>, <code>date_add_years</code>, <code>date_add_quarters</code>, <code>date_add_weeks</code>, <code>date_add_hours</code>, <code>date_add_minutes</code>, <code>date_add_seconds</code>, <code>date_add_millis</code>, <code>date_add_micros</code>, <code>timestampadd</code> (each returns a <code>DATETIME</code>; a <code>DATE</code> moves as its midnight), and a <code>DATETIME</code> moved <code>n</code> units: <code>years_add</code>, <code>quarters_add</code>, <code>months_add</code>, <code>weeks_add</code>, <code>days_add</code>, <code>hours_add</code>, <code>minutes_add</code>, <code>seconds_add</code>, <code>milliseconds_add</code>, <code>microseconds_add</code>, each with a <code>_sub</code> (<code>years_sub</code> …), plus <code>addtime</code>, <code>subtime</code>, and <code>period_add</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Format and parse</td><td><code>date_format</code>, <code>get_format</code>, <code>to_iso8601</code>, <code>jodatime_format</code>, <code>str_to_date</code>, <code>str_to_time</code>, <code>str_to_jodatime</code>, <code>to_tera_date</code>, <code>to_tera_timestamp</code>, <code>to_datetime_ntz</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Epoch</td><td><code>unix_timestamp</code>, <code>from_unixtime</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Time zones</td><td><code>convert_tz(dt, from_zone, to_zone)</code>, <code>from_unixtime(n, format, zone)</code></td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <Callout type="note" title="Time zones"><p>A zone is a fixed offset (<code>'+08:00'</code>, <code>'-0530'</code>, <code>'UTC+8'</code>, <code>'Z'</code>) or a tz database name such as <code>'America/New_York'</code>, which follows that zone's daylight-saving rules. Named zones are read from the TZif files under the <code>TZDIR</code> environment variable, or <code>/usr/share/zoneinfo</code> when it is unset. Windows has no zone files, so set <code>TZDIR</code> there to use named zones. A zone thinDB doesn't know makes <code>convert_tz</code> NULL, and <code>from_unixtime</code> renders it as UTC.</p></Callout>
  <Callout type="note" title="Current time"><p><code>now()</code>, <code>current_timestamp</code>, <code>localtimestamp</code>, <code>utc_timestamp</code>, <code>current_time</code>, <code>curtime</code>, and <code>localtime</code> return the statement-stable current <code>DATETIME</code>. <code>current_date</code>, <code>curdate</code>, and <code>utc_date</code> return the current <code>DATE</code>. Parenthesized and bare forms are accepted where applicable. These are parser-special substitutions, so they do not appear in the ordinary scalar registry.</p></Callout>

  <h3 id="json">JSON</h3>
  <p>Use the MySQL-style arrow operators for concise JSON navigation. <code>-&gt;</code> preserves the selected value as JSON; <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> returns its unquoted text representation. They are shorthand for <code>json_extract</code> and <code>json_value</code>, respectively.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" title="JSON arrow operators" code={`SELECT
  payload -> '$.customer' AS customer_json,
  payload ->> '$.customer.name' AS customer_name,
  payload ->> '$.items[0].sku' AS first_sku
FROM events;

-- Arrow extraction is valid in predicates.
SELECT event_id
FROM events
WHERE payload ->> '$.status' = 'paid';

-- Operators chain from left to right.
SELECT payload -> '$.customer' ->> '$.name' AS customer_name
FROM events;`} />
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Equivalent call</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>document -&gt; path</code></td><td><code>json_extract(document, path)</code></td><td>A JSON-typed value. JSON strings remain JSON strings and JSON <code>null</code> remains a JSON value.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>document -&gt;&gt; path</code></td><td><code>json_value(document, path)</code></td><td>Text. Strings are unquoted; numbers and booleans use their text form. JSON <code>null</code> becomes SQL <code>NULL</code>.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

  <p>Paths are rooted at <code>$</code>. Supported steps are <code>.member</code>, <code>.&quot;quoted member&quot;</code>, zero-based <code>[index]</code>, and <code>[&quot;quoted member&quot;]</code>.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" title="Supported path forms" code={`SELECT
  payload ->> '$.address.city',
  payload ->> '$."display name"',
  payload ->> '$.items[2]',
  payload ->> '$["key.with.dots"]'
FROM events;`} />
  <Callout type="warning" title="Path limitations"><p>Use a constant path for a query batch. Wildcards such as <code>$.*</code> and <code>$[*]</code>, recursive descent, slices, filters, and negative array indices are not implemented. Malformed or unsupported path syntax is an error.</p></Callout>
  <Callout type="note" title="Missing and invalid values"><p>A missing member/index, SQL <code>NULL</code> document, or invalid JSON document produces SQL <code>NULL</code>. For a selected JSON <code>null</code>, <code>-&gt;</code> returns JSON <code>null</code>, while <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> returns SQL <code>NULL</code>.</p></Callout>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>json_extract</code></td><td><code>json_extract(json, path)</code> returns JSON at a supported path.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_value</code></td><td><code>json_value(json, path)</code> returns a scalar as text.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_unquote</code></td><td>Unquoted JSON string content.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_valid</code></td><td>Whether text is valid JSON.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_type</code></td><td>JSON type name.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_length</code></td><td>Array/object length.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_contains</code></td><td>Document/candidate containment predicate.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_keys</code></td><td>Object keys as JSON.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_query</code></td><td>JSON at a path, as <code>json_extract</code> returns it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_exists</code></td><td>Whether a path selects a value.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_remove</code></td><td>The document without the values at one or more paths.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_quote</code></td><td>Text as a quoted JSON string.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>get_json_string</code> (alias <code>get_json_object</code>), <code>get_json_int</code>, <code>get_json_double</code>, <code>get_json_bool</code></td><td>The value at a path as <code>VARCHAR</code>, <code>BIGINT</code>, <code>DOUBLE</code>, or <code>BOOLEAN</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>to_json</code>, <code>parse_json</code></td><td>Validated JSON value from text.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

  <h3 id="network">Network addresses</h3>
  <p><code>inet_aton</code> and <code>inet_ntoa</code> convert IPv4 text to and from an integer; <code>inet6_aton</code> and <code>inet6_ntoa</code> convert IPv4 or IPv6 text to and from binary. <code>is_ipv4</code>, <code>is_ipv6</code>, <code>is_ipv4_compat</code>, and <code>is_ipv4_mapped</code> test an address.</p>

  <h3 id="nested">Arrays, maps, and lambdas</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Functions</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Build arrays</td><td><code>[a, b, ...]</code>, <code>split</code>, <code>array_generate</code>, <code>array_repeat</code>, <code>array_append</code>, <code>array_concat</code>, <code>array_flatten</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Read arrays</td><td><code>arr[i]</code>, <code>element_at</code>, <code>array_length</code>, <code>cardinality</code>, <code>array_slice</code>, <code>array_join</code>, <code>array_position</code>, <code>array_contains</code>, <code>array_contains_all</code>, <code>array_contains_seq</code>, <code>arrays_overlap</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reshape arrays</td><td><code>array_distinct</code>, <code>array_sort</code>, <code>array_sortby</code>, <code>array_reverse</code>, <code>array_remove</code>, <code>array_intersect</code>, <code>array_difference</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reduce arrays</td><td><code>array_min</code>, <code>array_max</code>, <code>array_sum</code>, <code>array_avg</code>, <code>cosine_similarity</code>, <code>cosine_similarity_norm</code>, <code>l2_distance</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Lambdas</td><td><code>array_map</code>, <code>array_filter</code>, <code>any_match</code>, <code>all_match</code>, <code>array_sort</code> with a comparator, <code>array_sortby</code> with a key lambda</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Maps</td><td><code>map&#123;k: v&#125;</code>, <code>map(k, v, ...)</code>, <code>map_from_arrays</code>, <code>str_to_map</code>, <code>m[key]</code>, <code>element_at</code>, <code>map_size</code>, <code>map_keys</code>, <code>map_values</code>, <code>map_concat</code>, <code>distinct_map_keys</code>, and the lambdas <code>map_filter</code>, <code>map_apply</code>, <code>transform_keys</code>, <code>transform_values</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Structs</td><td><code>row(...)</code>, <code>struct(...)</code>, <code>named_struct('name', value, ...)</code>, <code>s.field</code>, <code>s[i]</code></td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`SELECT
  array_map(x -> x * 2, [1, 2, 3]),                  -- [2,4,6]
  array_filter([1, 2, 3], x -> x > 1),               -- [2,3]
  array_sort([3, 1, 2], (x, y) -> y - x),            -- [3,2,1]
  transform_values((k, v) -> v + 1, map{'a': 1}),    -- {"a":2}
  map_filter((k, v) -> v > 1, map{'a': 1, 'b': 2});  -- {"b":2}`} />
  <p>A lambda is <code>x -&gt; expr</code> or <code>(x, y) -&gt; expr</code> and may read columns of the row. A map lambda takes the key and value. The column types themselves are described in <a href={withBase('/reference/data-types/#nested')}>Data types</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="bitmap">Bitmaps</h3>
  <p><code>to_bitmap</code>, <code>bitmap_empty</code>, <code>bitmap_from_string</code>, <code>array_to_bitmap</code>, <code>bitmap_from_binary</code>, and <code>base64_to_bitmap</code> build a bitmap. <code>bitmap_count</code>, <code>bitmap_min</code>, <code>bitmap_max</code>, <code>bitmap_contains</code>, and <code>bitmap_has_any</code> read one. <code>bitmap_or</code>, <code>bitmap_and</code>, <code>bitmap_xor</code>, <code>bitmap_andnot</code>, <code>bitmap_remove</code>, <code>bitmap_subset_in_range</code>, <code>bitmap_subset_limit</code>, and <code>sub_bitmap</code> combine and slice them. <code>bitmap_to_string</code>, <code>bitmap_to_array</code>, <code>bitmap_to_binary</code>, <code>bitmap_to_base64</code>, and <code>bitmap_hash</code> convert them back.</p>

  <h3 id="conditional-conversion">Conditional and conversion</h3>
  <p><code>coalesce</code>, <code>if</code>, <code>ifnull</code>, and <code>nullif</code> implement null-aware conditional expressions; <code>assert_true</code> raises an error on a false value, <code>uuid</code> returns a random UUID string, and <code>sleep(seconds)</code> pauses. Conversions include <code>to_tinyint</code>, <code>to_smallint</code>, <code>to_int</code>, <code>to_bigint</code>, <code>to_largeint</code>, <code>to_float</code>, <code>to_double</code>, <code>to_boolean</code>, <code>to_string</code>, <code>to_date</code>, and <code>to_datetime</code>. Use <code>CAST(value AS DECIMAL(p,s))</code> for a precision/scale-specific decimal conversion.</p>

  <h2 id="aggregate-functions">Aggregate functions</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Functions</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Core</td><td><code>count</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>count_if</code> (alias <code>countif</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Distinct</td><td><code>count(distinct x)</code>, <code>sum(distinct x)</code>, <code>avg(distinct x)</code>, <code>multi_distinct_count</code>, <code>multi_distinct_sum</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Boolean and bitwise</td><td><code>bool_and</code>, <code>bool_or</code> (aliases <code>booland_agg</code>, <code>boolor_agg</code>), <code>bit_and</code>, <code>bit_or</code>, <code>bit_xor</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Value selection</td><td><code>any_value</code>, <code>first</code>, <code>last</code>, <code>max_by(value, key)</code>, <code>min_by(value, key)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Statistics</td><td><code>stddev_pop</code>, <code>stddev_samp</code> (aliases <code>std</code>, <code>stddev</code>), <code>var_pop</code>, <code>var_samp</code> (aliases <code>variance</code>, <code>variance_pop</code>, <code>variance_samp</code>), <code>covar_pop</code>, <code>covar_samp</code>, <code>corr</code>, and <code>mann_whitney_u_test</code>, which returns JSON</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cardinality</td><td><code>count_distinct</code>, <code>approx_count_distinct</code>, <code>ndv</code>, <code>approx_count_distinct_hll_sketch</code>, <code>bitmap_union_int</code>. All of them count exactly.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Percentiles</td><td><code>percentile(x, p)</code>, <code>percentile_cont(x, p)</code>, and <code>median</code> interpolate; <code>percentile_disc</code> returns an input value; <code>percentile_approx</code> and <code>percentile_approx_weighted</code> return StarRocks' answer, computed exactly. The <code>WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY ...)</code> form is not supported.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Top values</td><td><code>approx_top_k(x, k)</code> returns the <code>k</code> most frequent values as <code>ARRAY&lt;STRUCT&lt;item, count&gt;&gt;</code>, counted exactly.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Collections</td><td><code>group_concat</code> (alias <code>string_agg</code>), <code>array_agg</code>, <code>array_agg_distinct</code>, <code>array_unique_agg</code>, <code>map_agg(key, value)</code>. <code>group_concat</code> and <code>array_agg</code> take an <code>ORDER BY</code> inside the call.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bitmaps</td><td><code>bitmap_agg</code>, <code>bitmap_union</code>, <code>bitmap_union_count</code>, <code>bitmap_intersect</code></td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>Any aggregate takes <code>FILTER (WHERE condition)</code>, and any aggregate can run as a <a href={withBase('/sql/windows/')}>window function</a>.</p>
  <Callout type="warning" title="Memory-sensitive aggregates"><p>Exact distinct aggregates, exact percentiles, <code>approx_top_k</code>, and the collection aggregates keep unbounded per-group state. Size the query and process memory budgets for their cardinality, or use a bounded query shape.</p></Callout>

  <h2 id="special-forms">Special SQL forms</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>EXTRACT(field FROM value)</code></td><td>Fields are <code>YEAR</code>, <code>QUARTER</code>, <code>MONTH</code>, <code>WEEK</code>, <code>DAY</code>, <code>DAYOFYEAR</code>, <code>HOUR</code>, <code>MINUTE</code>, <code>SECOND</code>, <code>MICROSECOND</code>, and MySQL's compound units such as <code>YEAR_MONTH</code> and <code>DAY_SECOND</code>. Other fields are rejected.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>CAST(value AS type)</code></td><td>Lowers to the appropriate typed conversion, including precision/scale-aware decimal casts. Text cast to <code>DATE</code> or <code>DATETIME</code> is NULL when it isn't a date. A number cast to an integer type is NULL outside that type's range, as in StarRocks: a LARGEINT past 2^63 - 1 cast to <code>BIGINT</code> is NULL, and so is a number argument outside the range of a function's integer parameter.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json -&gt; path</code></td><td>MySQL-style JSON extraction; lowers to <code>json_extract</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json -&gt;&gt; path</code></td><td>JSON scalar extraction; lowers to <code>json_value</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>read_csv</code>, <code>read_json</code>, <code>read_parquet</code></td><td>Table-source functions gated by the server's <code>--file-root</code>. They are not scalar calls.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

  <h2 id="wire-probes">Wire compatibility probes</h2>
  <p>These names are handled by the wire layer for client and pool discovery. They are intentionally narrower than ordinary scalar functions; rely on the standalone probe shapes shown below rather than embedding them in arbitrary expressions.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Wire</th><th>Accepted probes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>MySQL</td><td><code>version()</code>, <code>database()</code>, <code>schema()</code>, <code>user()</code>, <code>current_user()</code>, <code>session_user()</code>, <code>connection_id()</code>, plus documented <code>@@</code> server/session variables.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td><code>version()</code>, <code>pg_catalog.version()</code>, <code>pg_backend_pid()</code>, <code>pg_cancel_backend(pid)</code>, <code>pg_terminate_backend(pid)</code>, <code>current_database()</code>, <code>current_schema()</code>, bare <code>current_user</code>, <code>user</code>, and <code>session_user</code>.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

  <h2 id="resolution">Types and overload resolution</h2>
  <p>The registry resolves exact signatures first, then applies cost-ranked implicit numeric coercions. Decimal arithmetic receives precision/scale-specialized kernels. String-to-number conversion remains explicit: use a <code>to_*</code> function or <code>CAST</code>. Text where a function takes only a <code>DATE</code> or <code>DATETIME</code>, whether a literal, a column or another expression, is read as <code>CAST</code> to that type reads it, so text that doesn't read is <code>NULL</code>. So is a number there, as <code>YYYYMMDD</code> or <code>YYYYMMDDhhmmss</code>: <code>YEAR(20260131)</code> is 2026, and a number that isn't a date is <code>NULL</code>.</p>
